{# Storm Energy RTU -- Mosquitto ACL file (Phase 4 design)
   Generated by Ansible. Do not edit manually.
   Template: ansible/scanvix_rtu/roles/mqtt/templates/storm_acl.j2

   This file is only consulted by Mosquitto when `acl_file` is set on a
   listener, which currently happens ONLY on the 1883 listener, and ONLY
   when scanvix_mqtt_acl_enabled is true (see mosquitto.conf.j2). With the
   default scanvix_mqtt_acl_enabled: false, this template is never
   rendered into the live config at all.

   Design decision (Phase 4 report item C): explicit per-user `user` /
   `topic` stanzas, generated one at a time from scanvix_mqtt_devices --
   NOT the Mosquitto `%u` or `%c` pattern-substitution shortcuts.
     - `%u` would substitute the literal username (e.g. "rtu_864012049873321")
       into a pattern like `topic readwrite storm/meters/%u/#`, which -- because
       of the "rtu_" prefix this project's usernames always carry -- would
       produce `storm/meters/rtu_864012049873321/#`, NOT
       `storm/meters/864012049873321/#`. It does not match the real topic
       root, so %u cannot be used as-is for this username scheme.
     - `%c` substitutes the client-supplied MQTT Client Identifier, which
       is presented by the client at CONNECT time and is NOT
       cryptographically bound to the authenticated username. Even though
       the Phase 4 example has the client ID equal to the bare IMEI, using
       %c here would let any authenticated device choose an arbitrary
       client ID and thereby claim a different device's topic namespace.
       Rejected for that reason.
   Every device below therefore gets its own literal, pre-derived `user`
   line and literal `topic` lines -- no runtime substitution of untrusted
   client-supplied data is used to scope access. #}

# === Existing production credential: allipuram_ingest =====================
# PRESERVED, unrestricted, exactly as it behaves today with no acl_file at
# all -- enabling acl_file must not narrow this account's access. This
# stanza is NOT part of the per-device design below; it exists purely so
# that switching scanvix_mqtt_acl_enabled to true cannot silently break
# the current production MQTT path.
user {{ scanvix_mqtt_username | default('allipuram_ingest') }}
topic readwrite #

{% if scanvix_mqtt_bench_enabled | default(false) | bool %}
# === Bench listener (1885) credentials =====================================
# The bench listener (1885) does not currently set acl_file itself, so
# these stanzas have no effect unless/until that listener is also given
# an acl_file directive in a future phase. Included here only so the ACL
# file stays consistent with all known non-per-device credentials if that
# ever changes; access mirrors the bench listener's current unrestricted
# behaviour.
user {{ scanvix_mqtt_bench_username | default('bench_ingest') }}
topic readwrite #
{% endif %}

{% if scanvix_mqtt_backend_username is defined %}
# === Backend/service identity (future scanvix_mqtt_backend_username) ======
# PROJECT DESIGN (Phase 4 report item J): a future, separate credential
# for the Storm backend service, distinct from allipuram_ingest and from
# every per-device credential. NOT created by this phase -- this block
# only renders if scanvix_mqtt_backend_username is explicitly defined by
# a future phase. The backend subscribes to telemetry/status/attributes
# across all devices and publishes per-device cmd topics (confirmed
# against backend/app/mqtt/consumer.py and
# backend/app/commands/service.py); it does not need write access to
# telemetry/status/attributes, and does not need to read cmd (only the
# device does).
user {{ scanvix_mqtt_backend_username }}
topic read storm/meters/+/telemetry
topic read storm/meters/+/status
topic read storm/meters/+/attributes
topic write storm/meters/+/cmd
{% endif %}

# === Per-device RTU credentials =============================================
# One explicit stanza per device in scanvix_mqtt_devices, generated from
# already-validated device metadata (device_id is validated -- 15 ASCII
# digits, Scanvix IMEI format -- by app.protocol.device_identity /
# roles/mqtt/tasks/device_credentials.yml *before* it is ever added to
# this list; this template does not itself re-validate, it only renders).
#
# PROJECT DESIGN, not vendor-mandated (Phase 4 report item G): each
# device may WRITE its own telemetry/status/attributes and READ its own
# cmd topic only. No device may access another device's topic root, and
# no device gets the broker-wide "#" access allipuram_ingest has today.
{% for device in scanvix_mqtt_devices | default([]) %}
{% if device.enabled | default(true) %}
# Device {{ device.device_id }}{% if device.label is defined %} ({{ device.label }}){% endif %}

user {{ device.username }}
topic write {{ device.topic_root }}/telemetry
topic write {{ device.topic_root }}/status
topic write {{ device.topic_root }}/attributes
topic read {{ device.topic_root }}/cmd
{% endif %}
{% endfor %}
